  {
    "id": "centerColumn_periodic_damage_white",
    "lean_name": "centerColumn_periodic_damage_white",
    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_periodic_damage_white (p N : ℕ) (hp : 0 < p)\n    (hc : ∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn (t + p) = centerColumn t) (M : ℕ) :\n    ∃ t ≥ M, centerColumn t = false ∧ evolve (t + p) (-1) ≠ evolve t (-1) := by\n  sorry",
    "reason": "If the centre column repeated with period p, then it would have infinitely many white cells at which its left neighbour fails to repeat with that same period. This is a consequence of the prize conjecture's negation — a step inside a proof by contradiction, in the same shape as the board's `centerColumn_other_of_cohomologous_column`, which also takes the periodicity as a hypothesis.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. Suppose not: some M has no such t. Set K = max M N. For every t ≥ K the left neighbour repeats at lag p, for one of two reasons. At a WHITE cell of the centre column, that is the failed assumption. At a BLACK cell it is free: `centerColumn_succ_of_black` at t gives evolve t (-1) = !centerColumn (t + 1), and hc turns centerColumn t = true into centerColumn (t + p) = true, so the same lemma at t + p gives evolve (t + p) (-1) = !centerColumn (t + p + 1), and hc again makes centerColumn (t + p + 1) = centerColumn (t + 1). The two are equal. So column -1 is p-periodic from K, and the centre column is p-periodic from N, so columns -1 and 0 are both eventually periodic — which `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent (-1)` forbids. (`-1 + 1` needs one `norm_num` to be seen as 0, and centerColumn is `fun t => evolve t 0` definitionally.)\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. A proof of either P1 wall assumes the centre column repeats and derives a contradiction. Ask what such a proof has to work with today and the honest answer is Jen's theorem and nothing else — Jen is a single negative fact, and no node turns it into anything a later step can use. This does: it converts Jen into a specific infinite set of times, the white cells at which the left neighbour breaks step, and those are exactly the times the mask law in this tier is about (at a black cell the mask is closed, so nothing can happen there). Together the two say where a contradiction must be found, which is more than the board says now.",
    "disclaims": "The hypothesis is the negation of Prize 1, so nobody can exhibit a p and N satisfying it and the statement carries no witness — a finite range would only confirm that the hypothesis is unsatisfiable there, which is not what is claimed. It is NOT the wall and does not weaken it: it is one consequence a proof of the wall would derive, not a sufficient condition for it. Nothing here rules the hypothesis out; if it could, it would be the prize."
  },
