","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":56,"newStart":1,"newLines":68,"lines":[" {"proposals": [","   {","-    "id": "centerColumn_density_tendsto_half_iff_excess_nat",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_density_tendsto_half_iff_excess_nat",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_density_tendsto_half_iff_excess_nat :\n    Filter.Tendsto centerColumnDensity Filter.atTop (nhds (1 / 2 : ℝ)) ↔\n      ∀ d : ℕ, 0 < d → ∃ N₀ : ℕ, ∀ N ≥ N₀,\n        2 * d * ((Finset.range N).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card ≤ d * N + N ∧\n          d * N ≤ 2 * d * ((Finset.range N).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card + N := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "The board already has one bridge from the balance prize to arithmetic, centerColumn_density_tendsto_half_iff_excess, but it lands in ℝ with an absolute value over casts and a real ε. The captain's own rule says to state it in ℕ, and the record says why: two of the three budget exhaustions on 2026-09-07 were provers cycling on casts, and the two P2 nodes that were stated with |·| over casts (centerColumnCount_sandwich, centerColumn_excess_interpolate) each cost a ladder rung for a two-line fact. This is the same bridge with no reals and no absolute value anywhere: d is the reciprocal of ε, and the two ℕ inequalities are exactly |2 * count N - N| ≤ N / d multiplied through by d. Why a proof of centerColumn_density_tendsto_half would cite it: it is the last step. Every structural fact anyone could prove about the centre column is a fact about a natural-number count, so a proof arrives holding the right-hand side and needs this iff read right to left; and every intermediate P2 node after this one can be stated and proved entirely in ℕ, which is what iff_excess was landed to make possible and did not quite achieve.",","-    "disclaims": "Proves nothing about rule 30 — it is true of any Bool sequence in the centre column's place, and it is a restatement of the prize, not a step towards it. No finite witness is possible: both sides quantify over all large N.",","-    "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have main : ∀ c N d : ℕ, 0 < d →\n      ((2 * d * c ≤ d * N + N ∧ d * N ≤ 2 * d * c + N) ↔\n        |2 * (c : ℝ) - (N : ℝ)| ≤ (1 / (d : ℝ)) * (N : ℝ)) := by\n    intro c N d hd\n    have hd' : (0 : ℝ) < d := by exact_mod_cast hd\n    have cancel : (d : ℝ) * (1 / (d : ℝ) * (N : ℝ)) = (N : ℝ) := by field_simp\n    rw [abs_le]\n    constructor\n    · rintro ⟨k1, k2⟩\n      have k1' : 2 * (d : ℝ) * (c : ℝ) ≤ (d : ℝ) * (N : ℝ) + (N : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast k1\n      have k2' : (d : ℝ) * (N : ℝ) ≤ 2 * (d : ℝ) * (c : ℝ) + (N : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast k2\n      constructor\n      · have t : (d : ℝ) * ((N : ℝ) - 2 * (c : ℝ)) ≤ (d : ℝ) * (1 / (d : ℝ) * (N : ℝ)) := by\n          rw [cancel]; nlinarith\n        have := le_of_mul_le_mul_left t hd'\n        linarith\n      · have t : (d : ℝ) * (2 * (c : ℝ) - (N : ℝ)) ≤ (d : ℝ) * (1 / (d : ℝ) * (N : ℝ)) := by\n          rw [cancel]; nlinarith\n        have := le_of_mul_le_mul_left t hd'\n        linarith\n    · rintro ⟨k1, k2⟩\n      constructor\n      · have t := mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left k2 hd'.le\n        rw [cancel] at t\n        have : 2 * (d : ℝ) * (c : ℝ) ≤ (d : ℝ) * (N : ℝ) + (N : ℝ) := by nlinarith\n        exact_mod_cast this\n      · have k1' : -(2 * (c : ℝ) - (N : ℝ)) ≤ 1 / (d : ℝ) * (N : ℝ) := by linarith\n        have t := mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left k1' hd'.le\n        rw [cancel] at t\n        have : (d : ℝ) * (N : ℝ) ≤ 2 * (d : ℝ) * (c : ℝ) + (N : ℝ) := by nlinarith\n        exact_mod_cast this\n  rw [centerColumn_density_tendsto_half_iff_excess]\n  constructor\n  · intro h d hd\n    have hd' : (0 : ℝ) < d := by exact_mod_cast hd\n    obtain ⟨N₀, hN₀⟩ := h (1 / (d : ℝ)) (by positivity)\n    exact ⟨N₀, fun N hN => (main _ N d hd).mpr (hN₀ N hN)⟩\n  · intro h ε hε\n    obtain ⟨d, hdgt⟩ := exists_nat_gt (1 / ε)\n    have hpos : (0 : ℝ) < (d : ℝ) := lt_trans (by positivity) hdgt\n    have hdpos : 0 < d := by exact_mod_cast hpos\n    obtain ⟨N₀, hN₀⟩ := h d hdpos\n    refine ⟨N₀, fun N hN => ?_⟩\n    have hb := (main _ N d hdpos).mp (hN₀ N hN)\n    have hinv : 1 / (d : ℝ) ≤ ε := by\n      rw [div_le_iff₀ hpos]\n      rw [div_lt_iff₀ hε] at hdgt\n      nlinarith\n    have hN0 : (0 : ℝ) ≤ (N : ℝ) := Nat.cast_nonneg N\n    calc |2 * (((Finset.range N).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card : ℝ) - (N : ℝ)|\n        ≤ (1 / (d : ℝ)) * (N : ℝ) := hb\n      _ ≤ ε * (N : ℝ) := by nlinarith",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Prize", "Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnDensityTendstoHalfIffExcess", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","+    "id": "centerColumn_not_eventually_constant",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumn_not_eventually_constant",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_not_eventually_constant :\n    (∀ N : ℕ, ∃ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = true) ∧\n      (∀ N : ℕ, ∃ t ≥ N, centerColumn t = false) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The centre column takes both colours infinitely often. This is the period-1 case of Prize 1, proved outright: an eventually constant sequence is eventually periodic, so this is the smallest piece of the prize that can be closed today, and nothing on the board closes any piece of it. Both halves are contradictions against Jen's theorem, and neither needs anything unproved.\n\nBLACK HALF. Suppose centerColumn t = true for every t ≥ N. `centerColumn_succ_of_black` at t says centerColumn (t + 1) = !evolve t (-1); the left side is true, so evolve t (-1) = false, for every t ≥ N. Column -1 is then constantly white from N and the centre column constantly black from N, so both are eventually periodic with period 1 — which `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent (-1)` forbids. (That lemma's second column is `-1 + 1`, which needs one `norm_num` to be seen as 0, and centerColumn is `fun t => evolve t 0` definitionally.)\n\nWHITE HALF. Suppose centerColumn t = false for every t ≥ N. `rule30_eq` at position 1 and time t reads evolve (t + 1) 1 = evolve t 0 XOR (evolve t 1 || evolve t 2), and evolve t 0 is false, so evolve (t + 1) 1 = (evolve t 1 || evolve t 2). Column 1 can therefore never turn from black to white after N. Two cases. If evolve t0 1 = true for some t0 ≥ N then evolve t 1 = true for every t ≥ t0 by induction, so columns 0 and 1 are both eventually periodic, contradicting `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent 0`. Otherwise evolve t 1 = false for every t ≥ N, and then the same identity forces (evolve t 1 || evolve t 2) = false, so evolve t 2 = false for every t ≥ N too — columns 0 and 2 are both eventually periodic, contradicting `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_pair 0 2`.\n\nWhy a proof of the wall would cite it: the mask law and `centerColumn_periodic_damage_white` in this tier both reason about the centre column's white cells, and this is what says those cells exist at all. It is also the base case of the only ladder in the prize's direction that anyone can currently climb.",","+    "disclaims": "This is the period-1 case of Prize 1 and nothing more. It says nothing about any period above 1, and no induction on the period is implied or available — the two halves use completely different arguments and neither generalises. It is not a step toward either P1 wall.",","+    "witness": {","+      "expression": "((List.range 12).any (fun t => centerColumn t)) && ((List.range 12).any (fun t => ! centerColumn t))",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","+      "range": "t < 12 — a finite shadow only. Both colours occurring among the first twelve cells is a necessary consequence of the statement, so this catches a flipped quantifier or a swapped true/false; it is NOT evidence for the infinitary claim, which no finite range can carry."","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "centerColumn_density_tendsto_half_of_nearby_cuts",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumn_density_tendsto_half_of_nearby_cuts",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_density_tendsto_half_of_nearby_cuts\n    (h : ∀ d : ℕ, 0 < d → ∃ N₀ : ℕ, ∀ N ≥ N₀, ∃ M ≤ N,\n      d * (N - M) ≤ N ∧\n        2 * d * ((Finset.range M).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card\n            ≤ d * M + M ∧\n          d * M\n            ≤ 2 * d * ((Finset.range M).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card + M) :\n    Filter.Tendsto centerColumnDensity Filter.atTop (nhds (1 / 2 : ℝ)) := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "This is the shape of the last step of any proof of the balance conjecture that gets its information at particular window sizes rather than at all of them, and its conclusion is centerColumn_density_tendsto_half word for word. The hypothesis asks, for each d, that every large N have a cut M ≤ N where the excess is at most M / d and which is within N / d of N; then the excess at N is at most 3 * N / d, because centerColumnCount_sandwich says the cells between M and N move the count by at most N - M. Why a proof would cite it: no argument about rule 30 delivers a bound at every N at once — the automaton's structure arrives at special times, and this is what converts a bound at those times into the prize. It also fixes a claim the board is currently carrying loose. centerColumn_excess_interpolate's own docstring says a proof that controls the excess at a sparse set of sizes controls every size in between, and that is only true when the gaps are small compared with the sizes: at geometric cuts (n(k+1) = 2 * n(k)) the gap equals n(k), so d * (N - M) ≤ N fails for every d ≥ 2, and interpolation alone cannot bridge powers of two. This node is that sentence with the condition made explicit. Stated per-N rather than as a subsequence deliberately: it needs no monotonicity, no divergence, and no bracketing argument, and it is the weakest hypothesis the interpolation step can consume.",","-    "disclaims": "It does not prove the prize, and the hypothesis is not known for the centre column. It is true of any Bool sequence, and at M = N it degenerates to the excess bound itself, so its whole content is that the cut may be sparse. A neighbouring formulation is measured DEAD and must not be proposed: 'every block of some fixed length L is balanced to L / d' is unsatisfiable for the centre column, because long runs keep appearing — at L = 16 a block of 16 identical cells occurs at M = 22711, worst block excess 1.0000 (explorer/tessera2_blocks.mjs, N = 400000). No finite witness is possible.",","-    "route": {","-      "tactics": "  rw [centerColumn_density_tendsto_half_iff_excess]\n  intro ε hε\n  obtain ⟨d, hdgt⟩ := exists_nat_gt (3 / ε)\n  have hpos : (0 : ℝ) < (d : ℝ) := lt_trans (by positivity) hdgt\n  have hdpos : 0 < d := by exact_mod_cast hpos\n  obtain ⟨N₀, hN₀⟩ := h d hdpos\n  refine ⟨N₀, fun N hN => ?_⟩\n  obtain ⟨M, hMN, hgap, he1, he2⟩ := hN₀ N hN\n  obtain ⟨hs1, hs2⟩ := centerColumnCount_sandwich M N hMN\n  obtain ⟨g, rfl⟩ := Nat.exists_eq_add_of_le hMN\n  rw [Nat.add_sub_cancel_left] at hgap hs2\n  set a := ((Finset.range M).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card with ha\n  set b := ((Finset.range (M + g)).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card with hb\n  have ca : (0 : ℝ) ≤ (a : ℝ) := Nat.cast_nonneg a\n  have cg : (0 : ℝ) ≤ (g : ℝ) := Nat.cast_nonneg g\n  have cM : (0 : ℝ) ≤ (M : ℝ) := Nat.cast_nonneg M\n  have r1 : 2 * (d : ℝ) * (a : ℝ) ≤ (d : ℝ) * (M : ℝ) + (M : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast he1\n  have r2 : (d : ℝ) * (M : ℝ) ≤ 2 * (d : ℝ) * (a : ℝ) + (M : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast he2\n  have r3 : (d : ℝ) * (g : ℝ) ≤ (M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast hgap\n  have r4 : (a : ℝ) ≤ (b : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast hs1\n  have r5 : (b : ℝ) ≤ (a : ℝ) + (g : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast hs2\n  have hdg : (0 : ℝ) ≤ (d : ℝ) * (g : ℝ) := by positivity\n  have hmul1 : (d : ℝ) * (b : ℝ) ≤ (d : ℝ) * ((a : ℝ) + (g : ℝ)) :=\n    mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left r5 hpos.le\n  have hmul2 : (d : ℝ) * (a : ℝ) ≤ (d : ℝ) * (b : ℝ) :=\n    mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left r4 hpos.le\n  have expand : (d : ℝ) * ((a : ℝ) + (g : ℝ)) = (d : ℝ) * (a : ℝ) + (d : ℝ) * (g : ℝ) := by ring\n  rw [expand] at hmul1\n  have u1 : (d : ℝ) * (2 * (b : ℝ) - ((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ))) ≤ 3 * ((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ)) := by\n    have : (d : ℝ) * (2 * (b : ℝ) - ((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ)))\n        = 2 * ((d : ℝ) * (b : ℝ)) - (d : ℝ) * (M : ℝ) - (d : ℝ) * (g : ℝ) := by ring\n    rw [this]; linarith\n  have u2 : (d : ℝ) * (((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ)) - 2 * (b : ℝ)) ≤ 3 * ((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ)) := by\n    have : (d : ℝ) * (((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ)) - 2 * (b : ℝ))\n        = (d : ℝ) * (M : ℝ) + (d : ℝ) * (g : ℝ) - 2 * ((d : ℝ) * (b : ℝ)) := by ring\n    rw [this]; linarith\n  have hinv : 3 / (d : ℝ) ≤ ε := by\n    rw [div_le_iff₀ hpos]\n    rw [div_lt_iff₀ hε] at hdgt\n    nlinarith\n  have key : ∀ z : ℝ, (d : ℝ) * z ≤ 3 * ((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ)) → z ≤ ε * ((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ)) := by\n    intro z hz\n    have hz' : (d : ℝ) * z ≤ (d : ℝ) * (ε * ((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ))) := by\n      have h3 : 3 * ((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ)) ≤ (d : ℝ) * (ε * ((M : ℝ) + (g : ℝ))) := by\n        rw [div_le_iff₀ hpos] at hinv\n        nlinarith\n      linarith\n    exact le_of_mul_le_mul_left hz' hpos\n  push_cast\n  rw [abs_le]\n  constructor\n  · have := key _ u2\n    linarith\n  · have := key _ u1\n    linarith",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Prize", "Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnDensityTendstoHalfIffExcess", "Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnCountSandwich", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","+    "id": "column_neg_one_damage_mask",","+    "lean_name": "column_neg_one_damage_mask",","+    "statement": "theorem column_neg_one_damage_mask (X Y : Config) (t : ℕ)\n    (h0 : column X 0 t = column Y 0 t)\n    (h1 : column X 0 (t + 1) = column Y 0 (t + 1)) :\n    xor (column X (-1) t) (column Y (-1) t)\n      = ((! column X 0 t) && xor (column X 1 t) (column Y 1 t)) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "Two pictures that agree at the origin at times t and t + 1 differ at column -1 at time t exactly where they differ at column 1 AND the shared origin cell is white. A black cell at the origin erases the difference; a white one passes it straight through.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. `sideways_inverse` at position 0, applied to the row `evolveFrom X t`, says column X (-1) t = column X 0 (t + 1) XOR (column X 0 t || column X 1 t), and the same for Y. XOR the two equations. The h1 hypothesis cancels the first term outright. What is left is (a || b) XOR (a || b'), where a is the shared origin cell (by h0) and b, b' are the two column-1 cells: that is false when a is black and b XOR b' when a is white, which is exactly the right-hand side. A `cases` on a and on the two column-1 Booleans closes it; the only real step is aiming `sideways_inverse` at the right row, since `column X i t` is `evolveFrom X t i` and the lemma is stated about a bare `Config`.\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. Read it with X the seed and Y the picture grown from row p (`evolveFrom_evolve` identifies that with the seed's picture read p rows later). Then the hypotheses are exactly 'the centre column repeats at lag p at times t and t + 1', which is what the wall's hypothesis supplies, and the conclusion says: the failure of column -1 to repeat at lag p is the failure of column 1 to repeat at lag p, masked off at every black cell of the centre column. Jen's theorem forbids column 1 from repeating and forbids column -1 from repeating, and this identity is the only bridge on the board between those two facts. Any proof that a repeating centre column forces a repeating neighbour has to say what the mask does, and today nothing on the board says it.",","+    "witness": {","+      "expression": "(List.range 12).all (fun v => ([1, 2] : List ℤ).all (fun f =>\n  (List.range 9).all (fun t =>\n    !(decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 t\n        = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) 0 t) &&\n      decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 (t + 1)\n        = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) 0 (t + 1))) ||\n    decide (xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-1) t)\n        (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) (-1) t)\n      = ((! column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 t) &&\n          xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 1 t)\n            (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n              (decide (i = f))) 1 t))))))",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","+      "range": "twelve deterministic 'noisy' rows v < 12, each against itself with position f in {1, 2} flipped, at every t < 9. Evaluated true by `lake env lean` at seed time (explorer/seeder_p1_final.lean). NON-VACUITY CHECKED: within this exact range the left-hand side is genuinely nonzero at (v, f, t) = (0, 2, 6), (5, 1, 8), (5, 2, 7) and (7, 2, 6), so the witness exercises the branch that carries the content. The seed itself will not do here — its white left cone means a difference planted at position d takes about 4d rows to creep back to column 1, and over initialConfig with d ≤ 4 and t < 12 both sides are identically zero (explorer/seeder_p1_witness2.lean). Independently: 0 failures in 76,711 hypothesis hits over 400 random configuration pairs, 2,241 of them with a nonzero left-hand side (explorer/seeder_p1_damage.mjs)."","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "centerColumnCount_eq_stepMod_count",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumnCount_eq_stepMod_count",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumnCount_eq_stepMod_count (N : ℕ) :\n    ((Finset.range N).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card =\n      ((Finset.range N).filter fun n =>\n        ((stepMod (n + 1))^[n] (1 % 2 ^ (n + 1))).testBit n = true).card := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "The board's one bridge from the automaton to the arithmetic it has actually built machinery for — centerColumn_eq_rowNat_testBit, with rowNat_mod_eq_iterate beneath it — had zero dependents on 2026-09-10, which is the whole complaint of crystal 69. This is P2's side of that bridge: the number of black cells among the first N of the centre column is the number of n < N at which bit n of the n-th iterate of stepMod (n + 1) from 1 is set. Why a proof of centerColumn_density_tendsto_half would cite it: after this rewrite the balance conjecture is a statement about how often a bit of a truncated orbit of one bit-twiddle is set, with no cellular automaton, no cone and no configuration anywhere in it — and every tool the board has for that orbit (rowStep, stepMod, rowNat_agree_forward, stepMod_preperiod_of_return, step_two_mul) is stated in exactly that vocabulary. Any attack on P2 through the packed row opens with this line, and there is currently no way to write it down.",","-    "disclaims": "A change of vocabulary and nothing more: it says nothing about the value of the count. Crystal 69 records why the packed-row tools the board already holds do not simply carry over — they settle a FIXED bit index and this is the moving one, bit n at time n, which outruns the settling front (measured at about 1.25 n).",","-    "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have key : ∀ n : ℕ,\n      centerColumn n = ((stepMod (n + 1))^[n] (1 % 2 ^ (n + 1))).testBit n := by\n    intro n\n    rw [centerColumn_eq_rowNat_testBit, ← rowNat_mod_eq_iterate, Nat.testBit_mod_two_pow]\n    simp\n  simp only [key]",","-      "imports": ["Rule30.Proofs.CenterColumnEqRowNatTestBit", "Rule30.Proofs.RowNatModEqIterate", "Mathlib.Tactic"]","-    },","+    "id": "column_neg_two_damage_derivative",","+    "lean_name": "column_neg_two_damage_derivative",","+    "statement": "theorem column_neg_two_damage_derivative (X Y : Config) (t : ℕ)\n    (h0 : column X 0 t = column Y 0 t)\n    (h1 : column X 0 (t + 1) = column Y 0 (t + 1)) :\n    xor (column X (-2) t) (column Y (-2) t)\n      = xor (xor (column X (-1) t) (column Y (-1) t))\n          (xor (column X (-1) (t + 1)) (column Y (-1) (t + 1))) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "One column further left than the mask law, and there the mask disappears: the disagreement at column -2 is the plain discrete derivative of the disagreement at column -1. No masking, no `&&` — this is the one place in the leftward cascade where the relation is linear.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. `sideways_inverse` at position -1 gives column X (-2) t = column X (-1) (t + 1) XOR (column X (-1) t || column X 0 t), and the same for Y. XOR the two. The bracket is (u || a) XOR (u' || a), where a is the shared origin cell by h0 and u, u' are the two column--1 cells: it is false when a is black and the column--1 disagreement when a is white. The two cases then join, and that is the whole trick: when a is black, `column_succ_of_black` applied to X and to Y (they share the origin cell at t by h0 and at t + 1 by h1) pins both column--1 cells to the complement of the same cell, so the column--1 disagreement is false there too. So in BOTH cases the bracket equals the column--1 disagreement at t, and the identity is the stated XOR of it with the one at t + 1.\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. Under a centre column repeating at lag p, it converts 'column -2 repeats at time t' into 'the column--1 mismatch is the same at t and t + 1'. So column -2 is eventually periodic exactly when the column--1 mismatch is eventually constant — and eventually constant means either eventually zero (column -1 repeats at lag p) or eventually one (column -1 repeats at lag 2p). Jen forbids both. That is a genuine structural fact about the assumed picture, in the same currency as the mask law and one column further in.",","+    "disclaims": "The cascade does NOT continue, and this is the honest limit of the statement rather than a gap in it. The same claim one column further left — that the disagreement at -3 is the discrete derivative of the disagreement at -2 — is measured FALSE: 418 failures in 35,795 hypothesis hits over random configuration pairs (explorer/seeder_p1_damage.mjs). The reason is visible in the proof: at column -2 the second input to the `||` is still the shared origin column, and at -3 it is not. So a prover should not expect an induction here, and a seeder should not propose one.",","     "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 10).all fun n => decide (centerColumn n = ((stepMod (n + 1))^[n] (1 % 2 ^ (n + 1))).testBit n)",","-      "imports": [],","-      "range": "t < 10"","+      "expression": "(List.range 12).all (fun v => ([1, 2] : List ℤ).all (fun f =>\n  (List.range 9).all (fun t =>\n    !(decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 t\n        = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) 0 t) &&\n      decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 (t + 1)\n        = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) 0 (t + 1))) ||\n    decide (xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-2) t)\n        (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (i = f))) (-2) t)\n      = xor (xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-1) t)\n              (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n                (decide (i = f))) (-1) t))\n          (xor (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-1) (t + 1))\n              (column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n                (decide (i = f))) (-1) (t + 1)))))))",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","+      "range": "the same twelve noisy rows v < 12, flip f in {1, 2}, every t < 9. Evaluated true by `lake env lean` at seed time (explorer/seeder_p1_final.lean). Non-vacuity: the column--1 disagreement is nonzero at four (v, f, t) inside this range, so the right-hand side is not identically false here. Independently: 0 failures in 76,711 hypothesis hits over 400 random configuration pairs, 4,398 of them with a nonzero left-hand side (explorer/seeder_p1_damage.mjs)."","     }","   },","   {","-    "id": "centerColumnCount_block",","-    "lean_name": "centerColumnCount_block",","-    "statement": "theorem centerColumnCount_block (M k : ℕ) :\n    ((Finset.range (M + k)).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card =\n      ((Finset.range M).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card\n        + ((Finset.range k).filter fun j => centerColumn (M + j) = true).card := by\n  sorry",","-    "reason": "A proof of the balance conjecture has to be able to name the black count on a STRETCH of the centre column, [M, M + k), and not only on a prefix [0, N) — because every fact anyone can hope to prove about the automaton is local in time, and the count that the prize is about is not. The board can widen a prefix by one cell (centerColumnCount_succ) and can bound the widening (centerColumnCount_sandwich), but there is no way to say 'the count on this block' at all, so a block bound cannot even be stated, let alone summed. This is the identity that lets it be, and it is the supply underneath centerColumn_density_tendsto_half_of_nearby_cuts: the cut argument works one block at a time, and each block is this equation.",","-    "disclaims": "Says nothing about rule 30 — true of any Bool sequence. It is supply for a localisation argument, not a step of one.",","-    "route": {","-      "tactics": "  have key : ∀ (f : ℕ → Bool) (m : ℕ),\n      ((Finset.range (m + 1)).filter fun j => f j = true).card\n        = ((Finset.range m).filter fun j => f j = true).card\n          + (if f m = true then 1 else 0) := by\n    intro f m\n    rw [Finset.range_add_one, Finset.filter_insert]\n    by_cases h : f m = true\n    · rw [if_pos h, Finset.card_insert_of_notMem (by simp), if_pos h]\n    · rw [if_neg h, if_neg h]\n      omega\n  induction k with\n  | zero => simp\n  | succ k ih =>\n      have e : M + (k + 1) = (M + k) + 1 := by omega\n      rw [e, key centerColumn (M + k), ih, key (fun j => centerColumn (M + j)) k]\n      omega",","-      "imports": ["Mathlib.Tactic"]","-    },","+    "id": "column_damage_zero_of_black_run",","+    "lean_name": "column_damage_zero_of_black_run",","+    "statement": "theorem column_damage_zero_of_black_run (X Y : Config) (t j : ℕ)\n    (hagree : ∀ s ≤ j, column X 0 (t + s) = column Y 0 (t + s))\n    (hblack : ∀ s < j, column X 0 (t + s) = true)\n    (i : ℕ) (hi : i ≤ j) :\n    column X (-(i : ℤ)) t = column Y (-(i : ℤ)) t := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "A run of black cells in the shared centre column shields the left, one column per cell of the run. If two pictures have the same origin cell at times t .. t + j and that cell is black at t .. t + j - 1, then the two pictures agree at every position 0, -1, ..., -j at time t.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. Write D i s for the disagreement between X and Y at position -i at time s, and prove by strong induction on i the triangle claim: D i s is false whenever t ≤ s and s + i ≤ t + j. At i = 0 that is hagree. At i = 1 it is `column_succ_of_black`, which at a black origin cell pins the cell at -1 to the complement of the origin cell one step later; both pictures have the same origin cell at s (hagree, since s ≤ t + j) and at s + 1 (hagree, since s + 1 ≤ t + j), and hblack applies because s - t < j. For the step, `sideways_inverse` at position -i gives column X (-(i+1)) s = column X (-i) (s + 1) XOR (column X (-i) s || column X (-(i-1)) s), and the same for Y; XOR-ing, D (i+1) s is false as soon as D i (s+1), D i s and D (i-1) s are, and the triangle's index arithmetic supplies all three. Reading the claim at s = t needs exactly i ≤ j.\n\nWHERE THE WORK IS. The induction is two-step — the case i + 1 needs both i and i - 1 — so it wants `Nat.strong_induction_on` or a paired invariant rather than plain `induction i`. The triangle bound is the other half: carrying `∀ s, t ≤ s → s + i ≤ t + j → ...` rather than a statement at the single time t is what makes the step go through.\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. It is the quantitative form of the shielding the mask law shows at one column. Read with Y the picture grown from row p, it says: if the centre column repeats at lag p and has a run of j consecutive black cells at time t, then the j columns to the left of the origin all repeat at lag p at that time. So a proof that a repeating centre column forces a repeating neighbour cannot come from the black cells — it has to come from what happens between the runs. That is where the difficulty actually lives, and the board has never said so in a theorem.",","+    "disclaims": "`i ≤ j` is exact, not an artefact of the proof: the next position along genuinely differs. Over random configuration pairs the two pictures differ at position -(j+1) in 17,596 of 311,892 hypothesis hits (explorer/seeder_p1_runshield.mjs), and 7 times inside the witness's own range (explorer/seeder_p1_live.lean). So there is no stronger version to reach for. This is a statement about black runs only and says nothing about the times between them, which is precisely the open part.",","     "witness": {","-      "expression": "(List.range 6).all fun M => (List.range 5).all fun k => decide (((Finset.range (M + k)).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card = ((Finset.range M).filter fun n => centerColumn n = true).card + ((Finset.range k).filter fun j => centerColumn (M + j) = true).card)",","-      "imports": [],","-      "range": "M < 6 and k < 5, so every centre cell read has t < 10"","+      "expression": "(List.range 8).all (fun v => ([1, 2] : List ℤ).all (fun f =>\n  (List.range 4).all (fun j => (List.range 7).all (fun t =>\n    !((List.range (j + 1)).all (fun s =>\n        decide (column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 (t + s)\n          = column (fun i => xor (decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3))\n              (decide (i = f))) 0 (t + s))) &&\n      (List.range j).all (fun s =>\n        column (fun i => decide ((i * i * i + (v : ℤ) * i + 1) % 7 < 3)) 0 (t + s))) ||\n    (List.range (j + 1)).all (fun i =>\n      decide (column (fun x => decide ((x * x * x + (v : ℤ) * x + 1) % 7 < 3)) (-(i : ℤ)) t\n        = column (fun x => xor (decide ((x * x * x + (v : ℤ) * x + 1) % 7 < 3))\n            (decide (x = f))) (-(i : ℤ)) t))))))",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","+      "range": "eight noisy rows v < 8, flip f in {1, 2}, run length j < 4, every t < 7. Evaluated true by `lake env lean` at seed time (explorer/seeder_p1_final.lean). NON-VACUITY CHECKED in this exact range: 167 hypothesis hits, of which 7 have the two pictures genuinely differing at position -(j+1) — so the range contains cases where the shield is exactly as wide as claimed and no wider, and the witness would catch an off-by-one in either direction. Independently: 0 failures in 311,892 hypothesis hits over 600 random configuration pairs, at j up to 12 (explorer/seeder_p1_runshield.mjs)."","     }","+  },","+  {","+    "id": "centerColumn_periodic_damage_white",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumn_periodic_damage_white",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_periodic_damage_white (p N : ℕ) (hp : 0 < p)\n    (hc : ∀ t ≥ N, centerColumn (t + p) = centerColumn t) (M : ℕ) :\n    ∃ t ≥ M, centerColumn t = false ∧ evolve (t + p) (-1) ≠ evolve t (-1) := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "If the centre column repeated with period p, then it would have infinitely many white cells at which its left neighbour fails to repeat with that same period. This is a consequence of the prize conjecture's negation — a step inside a proof by contradiction, in the same shape as the board's `centerColumn_other_of_cohomologous_column`, which also takes the periodicity as a hypothesis.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. Suppose not: some M has no such t. Set K = max M N. For every t ≥ K the left neighbour repeats at lag p, for one of two reasons. At a WHITE cell of the centre column, that is the failed assumption. At a BLACK cell it is free: `centerColumn_succ_of_black` at t gives evolve t (-1) = !centerColumn (t + 1), and hc turns centerColumn t = true into centerColumn (t + p) = true, so the same lemma at t + p gives evolve (t + p) (-1) = !centerColumn (t + p + 1), and hc again makes centerColumn (t + p + 1) = centerColumn (t + 1). The two are equal. So column -1 is p-periodic from K, and the centre column is p-periodic from N, so columns -1 and 0 are both eventually periodic — which `not_isEventuallyPeriodic_adjacent (-1)` forbids. (`-1 + 1` needs one `norm_num` to be seen as 0, and centerColumn is `fun t => evolve t 0` definitionally.)\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. A proof of either P1 wall assumes the centre column repeats and derives a contradiction. Ask what such a proof has to work with today and the honest answer is Jen's theorem and nothing else — Jen is a single negative fact, and no node turns it into anything a later step can use. This does: it converts Jen into a specific infinite set of times, the white cells at which the left neighbour breaks step, and those are exactly the times the mask law in this tier is about (at a black cell the mask is closed, so nothing can happen there). Together the two say where a contradiction must be found, which is more than the board says now.",","+    "disclaims": "The hypothesis is the negation of Prize 1, so nobody can exhibit a p and N satisfying it and the statement carries no witness — a finite range would only confirm that the hypothesis is unsatisfiable there, which is not what is claimed. It is NOT the wall and does not weaken it: it is one consequence a proof of the wall would derive, not a sufficient condition for it. Nothing here rules the hypothesis out; if it could, it would be the prize."","+  },","+  {","+    "id": "centerColumn_eq_evolve_mul_pow",","+    "lean_name": "centerColumn_eq_evolve_mul_pow",","+    "statement": "theorem centerColumn_eq_evolve_mul_pow (k m : ℕ) :\n    evolve (m * 2 ^ k + k) ((m * 2 ^ k : ℕ) : ℤ) = centerColumn k := by\n  sorry",","+    "reason": "The centre column reappears verbatim, arbitrarily far out on the right: the cell at position m * 2^k after m * 2^k + k steps is the centre column's k-th cell, for every m.\n\nWHY IT IS TRUE. `rightDiagonal k j` is `evolve (j + k) j` by definition, so the statement is `rightDiagonal k (m * 2 ^ k) = rightDiagonal k 0` once `0 + k = k` is simplified. And `rightDiagonal_periodicFrom_pow` says `rightDiagonal k` is periodic with period 2^k from index 0 — exactly, with no onset and no transient — so `periodicFrom_mul` gives period m * 2^k from index 0, and reading that at n = 0 is the whole proof. The m = 0 case is the trivial one and is not vacuous cover: every m ≥ 1 is real content.\n\nWHY A PROOF OF THE WALL WOULD CITE IT. Nothing on the board relates the centre column to any cell away from the origin, and crystal 70 asks for exactly this as the opening move of the right-side route. The left-hand twin, `leftDiagonal_mul_pow_eq_settledCenter`, lands on `settledCenter`, a DIFFERENT sequence, because the left diagonals have transients and index 0 sits inside one; the right diagonals have none, so the same argument lands on the centre column itself. That difference is the whole of crystal 70's claim that the right side is a better position, and this makes it a theorem instead of a remark. Concretely it is a transfer principle: any statement about the cells (m * 2^k + k, m * 2^k), where the right-diagonal machinery is exact, is a statement about the centre column.",","+    "disclaims": "This is infrastructure, not an attack: by itself it proves nothing about either wall, and it is a short consequence of two closed nodes. It also does not touch obstruction 69's ratio — the centre column still outruns the left-hand settling front, and this says nothing about that. Its value is that it is the first node putting the centre column anywhere but the origin.",","+    "witness": {","+      "expression": "([(0, 1), (0, 2), (0, 3), (1, 1), (1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 1), (2, 2), (3, 1)] : List (ℕ × ℕ)).all (fun km => decide (evolve (km.2 * 2 ^ km.1 + km.1) ((km.2 * 2 ^ km.1 : ℕ) : ℤ) = centerColumn km.1))",","+      "imports": ["Rule30.Basic"],","+      "range": "the pairs (k, m) listed, so k ≤ 3 and m ≤ 3, the deepest being k = 3, m = 1 — cell 8 of row 11. Evaluated true by `lake env lean` at seed time (explorer/seeder_p1_final.lean). The list is explicit rather than a range because the cost is 3^(m * 2^k + k): k = 2, m = 3 is row 15 and already 14 million neighbour evaluations, and k = 3, m = 2 is row 19 and out of reach. Independently checked to k ≤ 6, m ≤ 4 — 35 pairs, 0 failures — with the array engine (explorer/seeder_p1_damage.mjs)."","+    }","   }"," ]}"]}],"originalFile":"{"proposals": [
  {
